    "result": "I implemented `search_products` in `solution.py` and wrote 51 pytest tests in `tests/test_solution.py`; `python -m pytest -q tests` now passes all of them. The query uses one of four fixed SQL statements, one per `(order_by, descending)` pair, with every caller value bound as a parameter. Name matching uses `instr(lower(name), lower(?))`, which avoids LIKE wildcards entirely, so `%` and `_` are literal. An empty `name_contains` is passed as NULL and skipped in the SQL. `limit` and `offset` are validated before the query runs, and both reject `bool` values even though `bool` is a subclass of `int`. Ties are broken by `id` ascending in both directions, and results contain only `id`, `name` and `price`. The first test run had four failures, all from my own wrong expected orderings in the tests (binary sort puts \"50% off juice\" first), so I corrected the tests, not the code. I did not run the coverage, CRAP, mutation or bandit gates, since they weren't part of the command you gave me.",
